# Copy to Makefile.local (gitignored) and edit. Included automatically.
#
# Everything here is per-operator: which Pulumi ESC environments hold your
# credentials, and which configuration file names your tailnet. None of it is
# portable to anyone else, which is why it does not belong in the repository.
#
# Use plain `=` rather than `?=`: Makefile.local is included before the defaults,
# so a plain assignment wins.

# Configuration file for the aws targets. Keep a personal tailnet name, state
# backend, or region out of the tracked config.yaml by pointing at a copy.
CONFIG = config.local.yaml

# Credential source. Leave unset to run the binaries directly with credentials
# already in your environment (what CI does).
#
# One environment supplies everything: it should `imports:` both a cloud-login
# environment and one holding the Tailscale OAuth client. Targets invoke it as
# `pulumi env run $(ESC_ENV) -- <binary> ...`.
#
# If that returns AccessDenied on sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity, the cloud role's
# trust policy does not admit this environment. ESC mints the OIDC token for the
# environment you OPEN, so a composed environment presents its OWN subject — the
# trust policy has to match it, typically with a wildcard over the ESC project.
ESC_ENV = your-project/your-launch-env

# Optional: defaults for the benchmark targets.
# FILTER = ^c6in\.large$$
# MAX_COST = 5
# MAX_TYPES = 1
